### What does CascadeType.ALL do?

`CascadeType.ALL` propagates all persistence operations from the parent entity to its associated children. Persisting the parent persists new children, merging merges them, removing the parent removes them, and so on.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

@Entity
public class Portfolio {

    @Id
    private String id;
    private String ownerName;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "portfolio", cascade = CascadeType.ALL)
    private List<Holding> holdings = new ArrayList<>();

    public void addHolding(Holding h) {
        holdings.add(h);
        h.setPortfolio(this);
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Holding;
import com.bank.model.Portfolio;
import jakarta.persistence.EntityManager;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class CascadeDemo {

    private final EntityManager em;

    public CascadeDemo(EntityManager em) {
        this.em = em;
    }

    @Transactional
    public void createPortfolioWithHoldings() {
        Portfolio p = new Portfolio("PF-001", "Rajesh");
        p.addHolding(new Holding("RELIANCE", 100, new BigDecimal("2450.00")));
        p.addHolding(new Holding("TCS", 50, new BigDecimal("3200.00")));

        em.persist(p); // CASCADE: inserts Portfolio + both Holdings
    }

    @Transactional
    public void deletePortfolio() {
        Portfolio p = em.find(Portfolio.class, "PF-001");
        em.remove(p); // CASCADE REMOVE: deletes Portfolio + all Holdings
    }
}
```

```text
Expected SQL:
persist(portfolio) cascades:
  INSERT INTO portfolios (id, owner_name) VALUES ('PF-001', 'Rajesh')
  INSERT INTO holdings (symbol, qty, price, portfolio_id) VALUES ('RELIANCE', 100, 2450, 'PF-001')
  INSERT INTO holdings (symbol, qty, price, portfolio_id) VALUES ('TCS', 50, 3200, 'PF-001')

remove(portfolio) cascades:
  DELETE FROM holdings WHERE portfolio_id = 'PF-001'
  DELETE FROM portfolios WHERE id = 'PF-001'
```

### What does orphanRemoval=true do?

`orphanRemoval` addresses a case cascade does not: the parent stays alive, but a child is disassociated. When you remove a Holding from the portfolio's list, orphanRemoval triggers a DELETE for that child at flush.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

@Entity
public class PortfolioWithOrphanRemoval {

    @Id
    private String id;
    private String ownerName;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "portfolio",
               cascade = CascadeType.ALL,
               orphanRemoval = true)
    private List<Holding> holdings = new ArrayList<>();

    public void removeHolding(Holding h) {
        holdings.remove(h);
        h.setPortfolio(null);
    }
}
```

```java
@Transactional
public void sellPosition(String portfolioId, String symbol) {
    var portfolio = em.find(PortfolioWithOrphanRemoval.class, portfolioId);
    Holding toSell = portfolio.getHoldings().stream()
            .filter(h -> h.getSymbol().equals(symbol))
            .findFirst().orElseThrow();

    portfolio.removeHolding(toSell);
    // orphanRemoval triggers: DELETE FROM holdings WHERE id = ?
    // The holding row is physically deleted because it was orphaned
}
```

```text
Expected SQL:
DELETE FROM holdings WHERE id = 42
-- The holding was removed from the collection -> orphan -> deleted
-- Portfolio still exists with its remaining holdings
```

### How do CascadeType.REMOVE and orphanRemoval compare?

| Scenario | CascadeType.REMOVE | orphanRemoval=true |
|----------|-------------------|-------------------|
| Delete the parent | Children deleted | Children deleted |
| Remove child from collection | Child stays (dangling FK) | Child deleted |
| Set child reference to null | Child stays | Child deleted |
| Parent still alive | No effect on children | Orphaned child deleted |

```java
// Without orphanRemoval: child stays as a dangling row
@OneToMany(mappedBy = "portfolio", cascade = CascadeType.ALL)
private List<Holding> holdings;

portfolio.getHoldings().remove(holding);
// holding row STAYS in the database with portfolio_id = null (or FK error)

// With orphanRemoval: child is deleted
@OneToMany(mappedBy = "portfolio", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
private List<Holding> holdings;

portfolio.getHoldings().remove(holding);
// holding row DELETED from the database
```

```text
Key difference:
- REMOVE: children deleted when PARENT is deleted
- orphanRemoval: children deleted when REMOVED FROM COLLECTION
```

### When should you use orphanRemoval?

Use it when the child has no meaning without the parent. This is a strong composition relationship, like an order and its order lines, a portfolio and its holdings, or an invoice and its line items.

```java
// GOOD: Holding has no meaning without a Portfolio (composition)
@OneToMany(mappedBy = "portfolio", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
private List<Holding> holdings;

// BAD: Student can exist independently of a Course (aggregation)
@OneToMany(mappedBy = "course", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
private List<Student> students; // WRONG! Removing a student from a course should NOT delete them
```

```text
Decision guide:
- Child cannot exist without parent? -> orphanRemoval = true (composition)
- Child can exist independently? -> NO orphanRemoval (aggregation)
- Child shared between parents? -> NO orphanRemoval (many-to-many)
```

### What are the pitfalls of orphanRemoval?

Replacing the entire collection triggers deletion of all old children and insertion of new ones, even if they are logically the same items. Prefer in-place mutation.

```java
// WRONG: replace the collection -- all old children deleted, all new inserted
@Transactional
public void updateHoldingsWrong(String portfolioId, List<Holding> newHoldings) {
    Portfolio p = em.find(Portfolio.class, portfolioId);
    p.setHoldings(newHoldings); // orphanRemoval deletes ALL old holdings!
    // Then inserts all new holdings -- wasteful churn
}

// CORRECT: in-place mutation -- only changed items affected
@Transactional
public void updateHoldingsCorrect(String portfolioId, List<Holding> newHoldings) {
    Portfolio p = em.find(Portfolio.class, portfolioId);
    p.getHoldings().clear(); // marks all as orphans
    for (Holding h : newHoldings) {
        p.addHolding(h); // adds new ones
    }
    // Or better: diff the collections and add/remove only what changed
}
```

```text
Pitfalls:
1. setHoldings(newList) with orphanRemoval deletes ALL old children
2. Reassigning a child to another parent deletes it (orphaned from first)
3. Clearing and re-adding same items causes DELETE + INSERT churn
4. Prefer in-place add/remove over collection replacement
```

### How do you keep both sides of the relationship in sync?

Helper methods on the aggregate root update both the collection and the back-reference, preventing stale state and ensuring orphanRemoval works correctly.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;

@Entity
public class Portfolio {

    @Id
    private String id;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "portfolio", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
    private List<Holding> holdings = new ArrayList<>();

    public void addHolding(Holding h) {
        holdings.add(h);          // inverse side
        h.setPortfolio(this);     // owning side
    }

    public void removeHolding(Holding h) {
        holdings.remove(h);       // triggers orphanRemoval
        h.setPortfolio(null);     // clear owning side
    }

    public List<Holding> getHoldings() {
        return Collections.unmodifiableList(holdings); // prevent direct mutation
    }
}
```

```text
Helper methods ensure:
1. Both sides updated atomically
2. orphanRemoval fires correctly
3. External callers cannot bypass the helpers
4. Collection returned as unmodifiable view
```

### What are common mistakes with cascade and orphanRemoval?

The most common mistake is using orphanRemoval on a shared relationship where the child can belong to multiple parents.

```java
// WRONG: orphanRemoval on a shared relationship
@ManyToMany
@JoinTable(name = "course_students")
private List<Student> students; // orphanRemoval would DELETE students when removed from course!

// CORRECT: no orphanRemoval for many-to-many
@ManyToMany
@JoinTable(name = "course_students")
private List<Student> students; // removing from course just removes the join-table row
```

```text
Common mistakes:
1. orphanRemoval on shared/reassignable children
2. Replacing the collection instead of mutating in place
3. Not synchronizing both sides of the bidirectional relationship
4. Missing cascade PERSIST when adding children through the parent
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer applies orphanRemoval only to true compositions, uses helper methods to keep both sides in sync, returns unmodifiable views of collections, and prefers in-place mutation over collection replacement to avoid wasteful delete-insert churn.

```text
Senior checklist:
1. CascadeType.ALL propagates operations from parent to children
2. orphanRemoval deletes children removed from a living parent's collection
3. Use orphanRemoval only for true compositions (child has no meaning alone)
4. Use helper methods to keep both sides synchronized
5. Return unmodifiable collection views to prevent direct mutation
6. Prefer in-place mutation over collection replacement
7. @Version (optimistic lock) on the parent updates when children change
```
